`MDN URL constructor TypeError invalid URL string behavior`

💡 Result:

When using the URL constructor in JavaScript, passing an invalid URL string—or an invalid combination of a base and relative URL—will cause the constructor to throw a TypeError [1][2]. This behavior occurs because the URL constructor is designed to strictly enforce valid URL parsing; if the input cannot be resolved into a valid URL, it halts execution by throwing an exception [1][3]. To handle this behavior safely, developers have three primary options: 1. Try...Catch: Wrap the constructor call in a try...catch block to prevent the thrown TypeError from crashing the application [4][3]. 2. URL.canParse: Use the static method URL.canParse(url, base) to verify if the parameters represent a valid URL before attempting to construct the object [4][3]. This returns a boolean value without throwing an error [4]. 3. URL.parse: Use the static method URL.parse(url, base), which returns a new URL object if the input is valid, or null if it is not [5][4]. This approach is often preferred as it avoids the need for explicit error handling and simplifies control flow [3]. The URL.parse and URL.canParse methods were introduced to address the inconvenience of the URL constructor's throwing behavior, providing a non-throwing alternative for validating and parsing URLs [5][3].

