`Zod 4 does .refine run after a failed z.url() format check in safeParse`

💡 Result:

Yes, in Zod (including version 4), a.refine check will generally run even if a preceding.url check fails [1][2][3]. Zod is designed to accumulate validation errors rather than short-circuiting on the first failure [1][2][3]. It distinguishes between "base type validators" (such as z.string or z.number), which are considered "non-continuable" if they fail because the type signature itself is incorrect, and "checks" (such as.url,.min, or.max), which are "continuable" [3]. Because.url is treated as a check, it does not mark the validation as fatal, allowing subsequent.refine calls to execute so that all validation issues can be collected and reported at once [1][2][3]. If you need a refinement to be skipped when a preceding check fails, you have a few options: 1. Use the abort option: You can modify the preceding check to stop the pipeline on failure by using the { abort: true } option, for example: z.string.url({ abort: true }).refine(...) [3]. 2. Use the when parameter: You can use the when parameter within.refine to explicitly control when the custom validation logic should execute based on the validation results [4][5][3]. 3. Manual validation: For more complex requirements, you can perform base validations with.safeParse and then manually run your custom refinement logic only if the initial parse succeeds [6].

